window.SKIM_PAPERS["1608.05859"] = {"concepts": [{"id": "input-and-output-embeddings", "name": "Input and Output Embeddings", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "A word-level neural language model holds two matrices of the same shape: the input embedding that maps a token id to a vector, and the pre-softmax projection matrix that maps a hidden state to one score per vocabulary word. This paper names the second one the output embedding and argues it is a word embedding in its own right.", "explanation": "Take a vocabulary of size $C$ and a hidden width $H$.\n\nThe input word arrives as a one-hot vector $c \\in \\mathbb{R}^{C}$. The input embedding $U \\in \\mathbb{R}^{C \\times H}$ turns it into the dense vector $U^{\\top} c$, which amounts to selecting row $U_k$, the vector for word $k$.\n\nThe network computes some activation $h_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}^{H}$ from that. A second matrix $V \\in \\mathbb{R}^{C \\times H}$ produces the logits $h_3 = V h_2$, which the softmax turns into next-word probabilities.\n\nLook at what a single row of $V$ does. Row $V_k$ is the vector whose inner product with the hidden state gives word $k$'s score: $p_t(o_t \\mid i_{1:t}) = \\frac{\\exp(V_{o_t}^{\\top} h_2^{(t)})}{\\sum_{x=1}^{C} \\exp(V_x^{\\top} h_2^{(t)})}$. Both matrices are $C \\times H$, so they are interchangeable in shape.\n\nThe paper's claim is that both encode word similarity, for different reasons. $U$ should give similar rows to synonyms, so the network reacts to them similarly. $V$ should give similar rows to interchangeable words, so their scores come out similar.\n\nStandard practice at the time was to treat only $U$ as \"the\" word embedding and discard $V$. The paper instead measures $V$ on word-similarity benchmarks, and reports that it is roughly as good as $U$ in word2vec skip-gram and better than $U$ in recurrent language models.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 1 (Introduction), notation for U, V, h2, h3; loss and softmax definition in Section 3; embedding-quality comparisons in Tables 2-3.", "ownerPaper": "1608.05859", "sources": {"paperId": "1608.05859", "note": "Section 1 (Introduction), notation for U, V, h2, h3; loss and softmax definition in Section 3; embedding-quality comparisons in Tables 2-3.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "weight-tying", "name": "Weight Tying (U = V = S)", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "Weight tying replaces the separate input embedding and pre-softmax projection with one shared matrix S used in both roles. It cuts the parameter count by one C-by-H matrix and, in the paper's language-model experiments, lowers perplexity.", "explanation": "Tying means enforcing $U = V = S$ with a single $S \\in \\mathbb{R}^{C \\times H}$. The same row $S_k$ then plays both parts: it embeds word $k$ on input, and it scores word $k$ before the softmax, so the logit for word $k$ becomes $S_k^{\\top} h_2$.\n\nThe constraint carries one structural requirement. The embedding width and the pre-softmax input width have to match; if they differ, an extra projection is needed.\n\nNothing else is added. There is no extra loss term and no stop-gradient. Gradients from both roles land on the same matrix and are simply summed, so one shared matrix is trained by two different signals at once.\n\nThe parameter saving is exactly $C \\times H$ weights — one whole matrix removed — which dominates model size when the vocabulary is large. The paper's large PTB language model drops from 66M to 51M parameters, and a decoder-tied translation model from 168M to 122M.\n\nIn the paper's language-model results, training perplexity rises while validation and test perplexity fall. It reads that pattern as less overfitting from the reduced parameter count.\n\nThe paper also reports a limit. Tying is harmful in word2vec skip-gram, where the hidden layer is the identity: if $p_t(i_t \\mid i_{1:t})$ is near zero, the norm of the embedding of $i_t$ is pushed toward zero as well. The recurrent layers in a language model decouple the two roles enough that this does not apply.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 3 (Weight Tying), the U = V = S definition; Section 4.2 with Tables 5-6; the word2vec caveat is at the end of the update-rule discussion in Section 3.", "ownerPaper": "1608.05859", "sources": {"paperId": "1608.05859", "note": "Section 3 (Weight Tying), the U = V = S definition; Section 4.2 with Tables 5-6; the word2vec caveat is at the end of the update-rule discussion in Section 3.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "tied-embedding-update-dynamics", "name": "Why Tying Helps: Update Dynamics", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "In an untied model, only the row of the current input word gets an input-embedding gradient each step, while every row of the output embedding gets one. The paper shows that in the tied model each row's update is the sum of the two, and that the result behaves mostly like the output embedding.", "explanation": "Work with the cross-entropy loss at step $t$, $L_t = -\\log p_t(o_t \\mid i_{1:t})$, with input history $i_{1:t}$ and target $o_t$.\n\nThe input-embedding gradient is nonzero only for the current input word. For $k = i_t$ it is $\\frac{\\partial L_t}{\\partial U_k} = \\left( \\sum_{x=1}^{C} p_t(x \\mid i_{1:t}) V_x^{\\top} - V_{o_t}^{\\top} \\right) \\frac{\\partial h_2^{(t)}}{\\partial U_{i_t}}$, and for every other $k$ it is $0$.\n\nThe output-embedding gradient touches every row. For $k = o_t$ it is $\\frac{\\partial L_t}{\\partial V_k} = (p_t(o_t \\mid i_{1:t}) - 1)\\, h_2^{(t)}$, and for all other $k$ it is $p_t(k \\mid i_{1:t})\\, h_2^{(t)}$.\n\nThe asymmetry bites hardest for rare words. Their input rows are updated only on the few steps where they appear, while their output rows are updated at every step.\n\nNow tie the matrices. Row $S_k$ receives the sum of the two terms.\n\nTake the two cases in turn. For $k \\neq i_t$ only the output term is present, so the update matches the untied output-embedding update with $U$ and $V$ replaced by $S$. For $k = i_t$ both terms are present, but the output term scales with $p_t(i_t \\mid i_{1:t})$, the probability that the current input word is also the target. That is near zero, because words seldom repeat back to back, so the input term dominates.\n\nThe paper's conclusion follows from those two cases: every row of $S$ is updated every step, and the tied embedding ends up closer to the untied output embedding than to the untied input embedding. It corroborates that with rank correlations of pairwise word distances, reporting Out-vs-Tied $\\rho$ up to 0.77 against In-vs-Tied 0.31-0.45.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 3, the two gradient equations for U_k and V_k and the case analysis for the tied matrix S; empirical check in Table 4.", "ownerPaper": "1608.05859", "sources": {"paperId": "1608.05859", "note": "Section 3, the two gradient equations for U_k and V_k and the case analysis for the tied matrix S; empirical check in Table 4.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "three-way-weight-tying", "name": "Three-Way Weight Tying", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "In an encoder-decoder translation model, three-way weight tying shares one matrix across the encoder input embedding, the decoder input embedding, and the decoder pre-softmax projection. It requires a single joint vocabulary built over both languages.", "explanation": "Start with the matrices a translation model holds. The decoder scores target words as $p_t(o_t \\mid i_{1:t}, r) = \\frac{\\exp(V_{o_t}^{\\top} G^{(t)})}{\\sum_{x=1}^{C_t} \\exp(V_x^{\\top} G^{(t)})}$, where $G^{(t)}$ is the decoder state. Here $U, V \\in \\mathbb{R}^{C_t \\times H}$ are the decoder [[input-and-output-embeddings]], and $W \\in \\mathbb{R}^{C_s \\times H}$ is the encoder input embedding, with $C_s$ and $C_t$ the source and target vocabulary sizes.\n\nDecoder-only tying sets $U = V$, exactly as in the language-model case.\n\nThree-way [[weight-tying]] (TWWT) goes one step further and sets $W$ to that same matrix. Source and target tokens are then embedded in one shared space, and one matrix serves all three roles.\n\nThis only makes sense if the two vocabularies overlap. So the model is trained on a single joint vocabulary, the union of the source and target vocabularies.\n\nThe paper obtains that overlap with subword segmentation. It learns byte-pair-encoding merges over the union of both languages' text — 89500 merge operations in its experiments — which leaves most subwords shared between the two languages: 85K shared out of about 94K for English-French, and 80K out of about 94K for English-German.\n\nReported effect: the three-way tied models have about 52% fewer parameters than the untied baselines, dropping from 168M to 80M for English-French and from 165M to 79M for English-German, with BLEU scores within noise of the baselines.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 3, the NMT model definition and the TWWT proposal; shared-subword counts in Table 1; parameter and BLEU comparison in Table 8.", "ownerPaper": "1608.05859", "sources": {"paperId": "1608.05859", "note": "Section 3, the NMT model definition and the TWWT proposal; shared-subword counts in Table 1; parameter and BLEU comparison in Table 8.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}], "items": [], "edges": [], "themes": [], "pages": [], "narrative": null, "insights": null, "refs": {"accessed": []}};
